<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <!--
            Least-privilege group for the tenant scheduler account (#347).

            THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. Binding the anomaly crons to a real,
            non-superuser identity is what makes Ring-2 licence enforcement
            apply to them at all — `env.su` bypasses Odoo's entire access-check
            machinery, so a superuser cron could never be denied. But a plain
            base.group_user cannot CREATE ncollection.alert: the only row
            granting create was base.group_system, and the scheduler must not be
            a system user (LicenseEnforcementMixin exempts `_is_system()` as
            well as su, so that would reintroduce the bug it exists to close).

            Without this group the fix "worked" in the worst way: the licence
            bypass closed, every detected anomaly discarded on every plan, and
            correct/broken indistinguishable because both look like no alerts.
            Caught by two reviewers; the branch failed its own CI traceback gate.

            SCOPE, DELIBERATELY NARROW. Create and write on ncollection.alert
            and nothing else. Not unlink: alerts are resolved, never deleted by
            the scheduler. This is the same shape as group_config_sync beside
            it — a service account gets exactly the one permission its job
            needs, never base.group_system.

            NOTE WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** GRANT: any right to READ the business
            models the detectors query (sale.order, account.move, hr.attendance,
            stock.*). Those stay governed by the tenant's ordinary role ACLs and
            therefore by its plan — which is the entire point of #347. This
            group covers only the scheduler's own bookkeeping.
        -->
        <record id="group_cron_service" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">NCollection Scheduler (service)</field>
            <field name="comment">Non-interactive tenant service account: may
                record anomaly alerts produced by scheduled detection. Not a
                system user, so plan entitlement still applies to everything it
                reads. Not for human users.</field>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
